                                                         owl:onProperty <https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_d088a3cb_d3e3_4eb2_9897_00aef0cb00cd> ;
                                                         owl:hasValue "0.0"^^xsd:double
                                                       ] ;
                                       <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "Milli ArcSecond"@en ;
                                       <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "MilliArcsecond"@en ;
                                       <https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_1f1b164d_ec6a_4faa_8d5e_88bda62316cc> "http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/MilliARCSEC" ;
                                       <https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_33ae2d07_5526_4555_a0b4_8f4c031b5652> "m''" ;
                                       <https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_7f1dec83_d85e_4e1b_b7bd_c9442d4f5a64> "mas" ;
                                       <https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9> """A minute of arc, arcminute, or minute arc (MOA), is a unit of angular measurement equal to one sixtieth (1/60) of one degree (circle/21,600), or π /10,800 radians. In turn, a second of arc or arcsecond is one sixtieth (1/60) of one minute of arc. Since one degree is defined as one three hundred and sixtieth (1/360) of a rotation, one minute of arc is 1/21,600 of a rotation.  the milliarcsecond, abbreviated mas, is used in astronomy.

-- QUDT"""@en ;
                                       <https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_c84c6752_6d64_48cc_9500_e54a3c34898d> "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minute_of_arc" .


