###  https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/chemical-substance#substance_5a4a9e9f_a1fe_42e6_8382_6a5edfa6b4a5
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/chemical-substance#substance_5a4a9e9f_a1fe_42e6_8382_6a5edfa6b4a5> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                                                                 rdfs:subClassOf <https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_12a9a254_9791_4a00_b045_f397bc3ab2bc> ;
                                                                                                 <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "Air is a ubiquitous and chemically relevant mixture that acts as a reactant, solvent, and environmental medium in many chemical processes. It is essential for representing atmospheric conditions, gas-phase reactions, and exposure contexts in chemical substance modeling. Including “Air” ensures consistent reference to a well-defined, commonly used mixture across domains."@en ,
                                                                                                                                            "Air should be modeled as a natural, variable-composition mixture rather than a pure substance, with potential extensions to specify dry air, humid air, or polluted air where needed."@en ;
                                                                                                 <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Air"@en ;
                                                                                                 <https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_26bf1bef_d192_4da6_b0eb_d2209698fb54> "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q7391292" ;
                                                                                                 <https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_6dd685dd_1895_46e4_b227_be9f7d643c25> "https://dbpedia.org/resource/Air" ;
                                                                                                 <https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9> "a homogeneous mixture of gases constituting Earth’s atmosphere, primarily composed of nitrogen, oxygen, argon, and trace gases"@en ;
                                                                                                 <https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_c84c6752_6d64_48cc_9500_e54a3c34898d> "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Air" ;
                                                                                                 <https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_fe015383_afb3_44a6_ae86_043628697aa2> "https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/C01214" .


