###  https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/chemical-substance#substance_f375bff6_79d0_472a_9469_393bf1dbfae2
<https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/chemical-substance#substance_f375bff6_79d0_472a_9469_393bf1dbfae2> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                                                                 rdfs:subClassOf <https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/chemical-substance#substance_dc68514f_b592_4e1b_a00f_fbb29f2fafbd> ;
                                                                                                 <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "SPEEK"@en ;
                                                                                                 <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "Sulfonated polyetheretherketone (SPEEK) is a chemically modified derivative of PEEK in which sulfonic acid groups are introduced to the aromatic backbone, enabling proton-conducting behavior. It must be explicitly distinguished from base PEEK due to its fundamentally altered ion transport, hydrophilicity, and membrane performance characteristics. Including this term enables precise representation of proton exchange membrane materials in electrochemical and polymer ontologies."@en ;
                                                                                                 <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "SulfonatedPolyEtherEtherKetone"@en ;
                                                                                                 <https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_4c8480cf_56de_41da_8699_f12a9033313a> "SPEEK should be modeled as a functionalized derivative of PEEK rather than a separate base polymer class. This distinction is important for accurately representing proton exchange membrane behavior in fuel cells and related electrochemical systems."@en ;
                                                                                                 <https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9> "A sulfonated derivative of polyetheretherketone (PEEK) containing sulfonic acid (–SO₃H) functional groups that enable proton conduction."@en .


